---
/**
 * /calculator — Spend-ROI calculator (May 2026 plan, Workstream C, sub-project C3).
 *
 * Editorial counter to a competitor that paywalls the same feature.
 * Ships free, transparent, with methodology visible above the fold of the
 * results. The calculator reads typed `earnRates` from L2 cards.json via
 * `getActiveCards()` — no LLM, no live scraping (Charter §6).
 */
import BaseLayout from "~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RewardsCalculator, { comparableCardCount } from "~/components/islands/RewardsCalculator";
import { getActiveCards } from "~/lib/cardsData";
import { VALUATION_SLUG_BY_LOYALTY_PROGRAM, dpValueFils } from "~/lib/valuations";
import { calculatorCardPayload } from "~/lib/calculatorCardPayload";

// The currencies this publication has published a baseline for, read from
// the same rows /valuations/ renders so the two can never disagree.
const publishedRates = Object.entries(VALUATION_SLUG_BY_LOYALTY_PROGRAM)
  .map(([programme, slug]) => ({ programme, fils: dpValueFils(slug) }))
  .filter((r): r is { programme: string; fils: number } => r.fils != null)
  .sort((a, b) => a.programme.localeCompare(b.programme));

const cards = getActiveCards();
const calculatorCards = calculatorCardPayload(cards);
const cardCount = comparableCardCount(calculatorCards);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="UAE credit-card spend-ROI calculator | DubaiPoints"
  description="Estimate rewards on UAE cards with comparable published rates. Enter your monthly spend across seven categories and see how it affects estimated AED return."
  fullWidth
>
  <article class="dp-article is-wide">
    <header class="dp-article-head">
      <p class="dp-article-eyebrow is-green">Tool · Calculator</p>
      <h1 class="dp-article-title">
        UAE card rewards calculator
      </h1>
      <p class="dp-article-lede">
        Estimate monthly rewards in AED from your spending and each card’s published earn rates.
      </p>
      <p class="dp-article-meta">
        <strong>{cardCount} of {cards.length}</strong> active cards can be ranked for general spending
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href="#methodology">Methodology</a>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href="/calculator/interest/">Interest &amp; payoff calculator</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    {cardCount < cards.length && <p class="dp-card-note">We only rank cards with a usable general earn rate and a published AED equivalent. Find other cards and their specific terms in the <a href="/cards/">card directory</a>. Unranked does not mean zero rewards.</p>}
    <RewardsCalculator client:load cards={calculatorCards} />

    <section id="methodology" class="dp-article-section">
      <h2>Methodology</h2>

      <p>These figures are estimates, not promised rewards. The tool uses the AED benchmarks below; actual redemption value varies. Some awards beat the benchmarks and others fall short.</p>

      <p>
        The calculator joins your stated monthly spend to each card's
        typed <code>earnRates</code> in our cards data file,
        category-by-category. The sliders cover seven categories so
        the calculator hits feature parity with the dimensions a UAE
        reader expects — dining, groceries, fuel, travel, online
        shopping, utilities and entertainment.
      </p>

      <h3>The per-card formula</h3>
      <p>
        Each card's monthly reward is the sum of seven products —
        spend in each category multiplied by that card's earn rate
        for the category. Categories the card does not bonus fall
        through to its base "everything else" rate. Utilities and
        telecom bills use the card's own utilities rate where it
        publishes a confirmed one, and fall through to the base rate otherwise.
        Products with held rates or unmodelled qualifying conditions are not
        return-ranked.
      </p>

      <h3>Start from a profile</h3>
      <p>
        The six profile buttons set the seven lines to round-number
        modelling assumptions — a grocery-led household, someone who
        eats out most nights, a frequent flyer, and so on. They are
        starting points to edit, not claims about any real household,
        and a figure on this page always states the spend it was
        modelled on. The default profile is the "Balanced" one.
      </p>

      <h3>Caps</h3>
      <p>
        Where a card publishes its cashback caps in typed form
        (<code>earnRates._caps</code>) — a monthly maximum, a
        per-category ceiling, or both — the calculator applies them to
        the month and the tile says the figure is the capped one.
        Held cap terms are not treated as confirmed inputs. Points and miles cards are not
        clamped: their caps are stated in dirhams and their reward is
        not, so there is no honest conversion. A card that needs a
        minimum monthly spend before it earns anything is flagged when
        your profile is below it; the figure is not zeroed, because
        issuers apply the rule differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the record: this section said from May 2026 that typed
        caps were applied. They were not, until 10 September 2026. The
        ADCB 365's AED 1,000 monthly cap and the ADIB Cashback's AED 300
        grocery cap were both credited without limit. Corrected the day
        it was found.
      </p>

      <h3>Fee break-even</h3>
      <p>
        On a card with an annual fee, each tile states the monthly spend
        at your category mix at which the reward first covers the fee's
        monthly accrual. It uses the uncapped rate, before waivers, and
        rounds up to the nearest AED 50. Where the card's monthly cap sits
        below the fee accrual, the tile says the fee is never covered by
        earn alone rather than printing a number.
      </p>

      <h3>Cards you already hold</h3>
      <p>
        Tick the cards in your wallet and the ranking narrows to them,
        with a table of which one to use for each category — the routing
        question a reader with two or three cards actually has. The table
        prices one category at a time from the same earn rates and the
        same published baselines, before monthly caps; the ranking beneath
        it still applies the caps to each card's month. Your selection is
        kept in this browser only.
      </p>

      <h3>The AED conversion table</h3>
      <p>
        Two separate things decide the AED figure on each card, and
        mixing them up is what made an earlier version of this page
        publish figures that were wrong by a factor of a hundred.
      </p>

      <h3>How much you earn</h3>
      <p>
        Cards state their earn rate in different denominators. Some
        quote a percentage of spend, some points per AED 1, Etihad
        Guest co-brands miles per AED 10, and the Skywards and Bonvoy
        cards points per <strong>USD</strong> 1. Each card's rate is
        applied through its own denominator, read from the earn-unit
        wording published on the card's page. A card whose wording
        does not state one is left out of the ranking rather than
        guessed at.
      </p>

      <h3>What a unit is worth</h3>
      <p>
        Cashback cards pay dirhams, so no conversion applies. For the
        currencies below we use the baseline published on our
        <a href="/valuations/">valuations page</a> — the same figure
        the card reviews use, not a separate number kept here.
      </p>

      <table class="dp-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Currency</th>
            <th>Fils per unit</th>
            <th>Basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {publishedRates.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.programme}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.fils.toFixed(1)}</td>
              <td>Published on /valuations/</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          <tr>
            <td>Every other currency</td>
            <td class="num">—</td>
            <td>No published equivalent; not ranked</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        If we haven’t published an AED equivalent for a currency, we
        don’t estimate its AED return or put it in the ranking. Read the
        card review for earning and redemption terms.
      </p>

      <h3>Annual fee handling</h3>
      <p>
        The default view shows monthly reward AED and annual fee
        separately. Toggle the "show net of annual fee" checkbox to
        subtract the known VAT-inclusive annual fee divided by twelve
        from the reward and re-rank. Records with held annual-fee or VAT
        terms are not net-ranked; their gross reward may still be shown.
        A fee-free card with a modest
        earn rate often beats a premium card once the fee lands.
      </p>

      <h3>Verification dates</h3>
      <p>
        Every ranked card carries its source-record date. Unresolved terms
        are labelled as pending rather than fully verified.
        Source records older than 90 days get a "data drift risk"
        chip — we do not hide them, but we tell you the figures may
        have moved.
      </p>

      <h3>What this calculator does not do</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          We do not score welcome bonuses. A card's first-year value
          can dwarf its earn rate; check the review page.
        </li>
        <li>
          We do not factor in lounge access, cinema BOGO, golf, the
          Entertainer, or hotel discounts. Use the
          <a href="/cards/">cards directory</a> filters for those.
        </li>
        <li>
          We do not check your eligibility. Each result tile links
          to the full card review with salary band and salary-transfer
          requirements.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <footer class="dp-article-foot">
      <p>
        Found a card whose earn rate looks wrong? Email
        <a href="mailto:corrections@dubaipoints.ae?subject=Calculator+correction">corrections@dubaipoints.ae</a>
        or open an issue on the data file. Every fix gets attributed on our
        <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
